1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,086
Longevity escape velocity, or LEV,

2
00:00:03,086 --> 00:00:09,837
is the state in which medicine extends a person’s remaining life expectancy at least as fast as they age.

3
00:00:10,337 --> 00:00:14,705
Here is what that would take, according to a preliminary evidence review.

4
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:22,145
As you age, your remaining life expectancy normally shrinks.

5
00:00:22,145 --> 00:00:29,138
In the model, a 70-year-old has about 15.6 years left; a year later, about 15.1.

6
00:00:29,638 --> 00:00:37,387
On the report’s main definition, escape velocity means progress is fast enough that this stops: one year older,

7
00:00:37,387 --> 00:00:41,435
one year later, with at least as many expected years left.

8
00:00:41,936 --> 00:00:47,597
That would not make anyone immortal. It only means the expected end stops getting closer.

9
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:54,509
Death rates climb steeply with age, roughly doubling every eight years.

10
00:00:54,509 --> 00:00:57,759
On a log scale, that is close to a straight line.

11
00:00:58,259 --> 00:01:04,488
Shift that line three years to the right and death rates drop by about 23% at every age.

12
00:01:04,488 --> 00:01:07,036
That is three age-years of progress.

13
00:01:07,537 --> 00:01:15,905
On the report’s main definition, escape velocity needs one age-year of progress every calendar year:

14
00:01:15,905 --> 00:01:21,847
death rates falling about 8% a year (7–9%), at every age from 50 to 90.

15
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:36,029
Compare that with history. Since 1900, overall death rates are reported to have fallen about 1–2% a year.

16
00:01:36,529 --> 00:01:43,123
On the report’s main definition, the required pace is four to eight times faster than the observed all-cause pace.

17
00:01:43,623 --> 00:01:49,230
Other definitions ask for between about 4% and 17% a year, at ages 50 to 80.

18
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:53,362
Whichever you choose, the pace has to rise several-fold.

19
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,222
What has medicine actually delivered?

20
00:02:04,222 --> 00:02:09,525
The best-demonstrated effects on mortality come from blood pressure, cholesterol,

21
00:02:09,525 --> 00:02:12,143
and some diabetes and weight-loss drugs.

22
00:02:12,643 --> 00:02:19,628
Each is a one-off shift worth roughly one to five age-years, mostly in people who already have a disease.

23
00:02:19,628 --> 00:02:21,358
A step up, not a new rate.

24
00:02:21,858 --> 00:02:28,014
To hold one age-year a year, a new step of that size would have to arrive every one to five years,

25
00:02:28,014 --> 00:02:30,840
and stack on the last. Nothing shows they do.

26
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:33,285
Even that is not enough.

27
00:02:33,285 --> 00:02:41,059
Mouse-scale steps of 20 age-years every 20 years pass the year-by-year test in only 5% of years,

28
00:02:41,059 --> 00:02:42,841
or 22% with smoothing.

29
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:55,333
Lab results look more promising. In mice, the best regimens extend median lifespan by a quarter to a third,

30
00:02:55,333 --> 00:02:58,924
though often in one sex or against earlier cohorts.

31
00:02:59,424 --> 00:03:06,740
If gains like that carried over to people in full, which nothing shows, they would be worth up to about 27 age-years.

32
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,555
That is an upper bound, not a finding.

33
00:03:09,555 --> 00:03:16,438
No intervention presented as targeting ageing has yet shown a human mortality benefit in the programmes reviewed.

34
00:03:18,633 --> 00:03:23,789
There is a catch. Suppose part of age-related risk resists every treatment.

35
00:03:24,289 --> 00:03:34,214
In the model, if 5% resists and never improves, a no-shrink escape lasts about 12 years. At 10%, about 2.

36
00:03:34,214 --> 00:03:36,955
At 20%, none within 60 years.

37
00:03:37,456 --> 00:03:40,843
The brain is the obvious candidate for stubborn risk.

38
00:03:40,843 --> 00:03:45,571
The report treats it as the hard case: a labelled judgment, not a finding.

39
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:58,819
So when? The evidence cannot separate four outcomes: stagnation, acceleration that falls short of escape,

40
00:03:58,819 --> 00:04:02,093
escape starting by 2060, or a later start.

41
00:04:02,593 --> 00:04:05,641
The model shows what each date demands.

42
00:04:05,641 --> 00:04:13,456
For a start around 2035, the pace must climb from 1.5% a year to about 8% within roughly nine years,

43
00:04:13,456 --> 00:04:15,723
then keep at least that pace.

44
00:04:16,223 --> 00:04:21,499
Nothing in this capped search shows a route to that, so the author judges it unsupported.

45
00:04:21,499 --> 00:04:24,285
That is a labelled judgment, not a measurement.

46
00:04:24,785 --> 00:04:27,834
Later dates depend on steps not yet seen.

47
00:04:27,834 --> 00:04:34,899
Neither “by 2060” nor “not this century” is contradicted, and the report offers no probability.

48
00:04:38,633 --> 00:04:43,930
For one person, it is a race. Suppose escape began thirty years from now.

49
00:04:44,430 --> 00:04:58,063
Someone who is 40 today would have about an 84% chance of being alive for it. At 50, 67%. At 60, 39%. At 70, 11%.

50
00:04:58,563 --> 00:05:04,469
These depend on the assumed mortality level, and on an onset the evidence does not show.

51
00:05:04,469 --> 00:05:06,751
Age at onset matters a great deal.

52
00:05:06,751 --> 00:05:13,462
Separately, the best-supported levers today are ordinary prevention, not a route to escape velocity.

53
00:05:15,667 --> 00:05:17,724
How far should you trust this?

54
00:05:17,724 --> 00:05:23,756
Confidence is high in the arithmetic, low to moderate in the evidence, and low on dates.

55
00:05:23,756 --> 00:05:29,514
It is a preliminary review built from web-search summaries, not the original papers.

56
00:05:30,014 --> 00:05:37,554
AI agents from a single model family did every step, and no human has checked a figure, a citation or a calculation.

57
00:05:38,055 --> 00:05:44,403
What would change the conclusions: a human mortality result for a treatment that targets ageing itself,

58
00:05:44,403 --> 00:05:47,114
and better mortality data by age and decade.

